[Line "3...c5 4.dxc5 e6 5.a3: main line"]
[Section "3...c5: the Botvinnik-Carls (Arkell-Khenkin) Variation"]
[Summary "White takes on c5 and uses a3 and b4 to gain time on the bishop. The set-up Bb2, Bd3, castles and Re1 overprotects e5 against ...f6 and ...Ng6."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 { [%cal Rc8g4] Black hits d4 before White has developed and keeps the bishop
flexible: if allowed, it goes to g4, a better square than f5. Botvinnik used the move against Tal in 1961;
Arkell and Khenkin made it respectable. }
4. dxc5 { [%csl Ye5] The critical move and by far the most popular. White gives up the pawn that supports e5 but
wins time: Black has to spend moves regaining c5, and with ...e6 the c8-bishop is shut in after all. 4.Nf3 is
the main alternative, which Johnsen and Hansen consider the most critical; it is sharper and needs more
theory. }
( 4. c3?! { The French move is what Black hopes for. } 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 Bg4 { A French Advance with the bishop
outside the chain and pinning the defender of d4: the lost tempo does not matter (Silman, quoting Houska). } )
4... e6 { Black shuts the bishop in voluntarily in order to win the pawn back with the other one. 4...Nc6 is the
next line. }
5. a3 { [%cal Gb2b4,Gc1b2] Dreev's choice, and the most popular move in practice. It prepares b4, which hits the
bishop as soon as it arrives on c5, and the bishop on b2 then protects e5. The obvious 5.Be3 holds the pawn for
a while (Efimenko recommends it) but the bishop gets in the way there; 5.Nf3 Bxc5 6.a3 comes to the same
thing. }
5... Bxc5
( 5... Nc6 { Developing first changes nothing. } 6. Nf3 Bxc5 7. b4 )
6. Nf3 { 6.b4 first is more common and usually transposes. 6.Qg4 is an independent and sharper try, attacking g7
before Black has castled; Idani's repertoire for Black gives it a chapter of its own. }
6... Nc6
( 6... f6 { The immediate attack on e5, the main line of Idani's repertoire for Black. } 7. b4 Be7 8. Bb2 a5
9. b5 Nd7 10. Bd3 { White keeps e5 overprotected and does not take on f6 voluntarily, and the pawn on b5 takes c6
from Black's knight. Black usually continues ...Nh6-f7 to add pressure; White castles and supports the pawn
with Re1 and Qe2. } )
7. b4 { With tempo: the queenside pawns gain space and b2 is cleared for the bishop. } 7... Bb6 { The active
square, aiming at f2 and at d4 once e5 comes under fire. }
( 7... Be7 { The same plan from e7. } 8. Bb2 f6 9. Bd3 Nh6 10. O-O { The knight goes to f7 to attack e5 a third
time. White holds the pawn with Re1 and Qe2; if Black ever takes on e5, a piece recaptures and the e5-square
belongs to White. } )
8. Bb2 Nge7
( 8... f6 { Black can also attack the pawn at once. } 9. Bd3 { White develops and lets Black take: after
...fxe5 the knight recaptures and e5 becomes a square for the pieces. } )
9. Bd3 { The bishop takes the b1-h7 diagonal unopposed, a small echo of the 3...Bf5 lines, where Black would
exchange it. } 9... Ng6 { The standard manoeuvre: the knight attacks e5 and can jump to f4. }
10. O-O O-O 11. Re1 { **Plan:** e5 is overprotected, so ...f6 only opens lines for White's pieces. Nbd2 and c4
come next, opening the long diagonal and the c-file, and g3 keeps the knight out of f4 if necessary.

**Verdict:** a normal, healthy edge: more space, the wedge on e5, and Black's c8-bishop is still a problem
piece. Black's counterplay is ...f6 and ...a5; neither should be ignored, and neither equalises on its own. } *

[Line "3...c5 4.dxc5 Nc6 5.Nf3 Bg4"]
[Section "3...c5: the Botvinnik-Carls (Arkell-Khenkin) Variation"]
[Summary "Black develops the bishop to g4 before ...e6 and wins the e5-pawn. White keeps c5 with c3 and Be3 and gets the bishop pair and a queenside majority."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. dxc5 Nc6 { Black develops first and wants the bishop on g4 before playing ...e6:
the pawn on e5 is then attacked twice and its defender pinned. Efimenko calls the move dubious and answers
5.f4; the natural 5.Nf3 is Dreev's repertoire and is followed here. }
5. Nf3 Bg4 { The point. If Black now gets ...e6 and ...Bxc5 in, it is a French with the good bishop outside, so
White has to do something about c5. }
( 5... e6 { Black changes his mind about the bishop. } 6. a3 { Back in the main line with 5.a3, since
6...Bxc5 7.b4 follows. } )
6. c3 { [%cal Gb2b4,Gc1e3] The solid way to keep the pawn: Be3 and b4 will defend c5. White accepts that e5 will
fall and takes the bishop pair and the queenside majority for it. }
( 6. Bb5 { The sharp alternative, covered at length by Johnsen and Hansen. White keeps the pawn with b4, but
after ...Bxf3 gxf3 the structure is ragged and both kings are exposed. Rich, and much more to learn. }
6... Qa5+ 7. Nc3 e6 8. Be3 )
6... e6 7. Be3 { Protects c5; 7.b4 is the alternative. Black now chooses how to win e5. }
7... Bxf3
( 7... a6 { Ruling out the check on b5 before taking. } 8. Nbd2 Nxe5 9. Be2 { The pin is broken and White
castles next. Material is level; the c5-pawn cramps Black and b4 will support it. } )
( 7... Nge7 { Aiming at the bishop on e3 before taking the pawn. } 8. Nbd2 Nf5 9. Bd4 { The bishop keeps both c5
and e5 covered, and Black has to exchange the knight to make progress. } )
8. Qxf3 Nxe5 9. Bb5+ { A check that gains time: the knight has to go back to block. } 9... Nc6 10. c4
{ [%csl Rd5,Rc6] White strikes at d5 while the pin on c6 lasts. 10...dxc4 11.Bxc6+ bxc6 12.Qxc6+ drives the king
to e7.

**Verdict:** White has the two bishops, the more active pieces and a mobile queenside majority led by the
c5-pawn. Black has an extra centre pawn and needs two more moves to castle. Practice favours White. } *
